--- body ---
## Source
First production run of the 0198 rewrite (aedist PR #689, 2026-06-04),
observed live. The core fixes held: close commit pushed unconditionally
(no strand), close durable. But the merge step failed through BOTH
boundaries and needed manual recovery.

## Context
Two distinct races share the --auto failure path:

1. `gh pr merge --auto` was called immediately after the close-commit
   push, while GitHub recomputed mergeability -> "Pull Request is not
   mergeable (mergePullRequest)". This is transient, but the exit-code
   fallback cannot distinguish it from "auto-merge not allowed", so the
   script wrongly concluded auto-merge was unavailable.
2. The fallback's `gh pr checks --watch` then hit the check-registration
   race ("no checks reported on the branch") and exited non-zero
   instantly -- die()'d with a misleading "did not pass within 600s"
   message (it failed in ~1s).

Manual recovery was trivial (watch the re-registered run, plain merge),
which is exactly what the script should do itself.

## Fix sketch
- Before (or on failure of) the --auto call: poll `gh pr view --json
  mergeable` until it leaves UNKNOWN (bounded, e.g. 5 x 10s). Retry
  --auto once after the recompute settles; only a second failure means
  auto-merge is genuinely unavailable.
- In the fallback: treat "no checks reported" as registration-race --
  bounded retry of the watch (e.g. 3 x 10s) before dying; fix the die
  message to reflect the actual failure (registration vs timeout vs red).
- Consider whether the pre-close PENDING gate (Step 0) should also queue
  via --auto instead of bouncing -- it fired on PR #689's fresh push and
  cost one manual round-trip too.

## Exit criteria
- [x] --auto retried after mergeability recompute settles; fallback only
      on genuine auto-merge-unavailable
- [x] Fallback watch survives the no-checks-reported registration race
- [x] die() messages name the actual failure cause
- [x] tests cover both races (stub: first --auto fails "not mergeable",
      second succeeds; checks watch fails once with no-checks then passes)
